# Install manually as /etc/pam.d/q-sunshine-remote (mode 0644).
#
# Delegate to the host's normal Debian PAM policy instead of bypassing it with
# a direct pam_unix rule. This retains locally configured SSSD/LDAP, faillock,
# access-time, and account-expiry policy in common-auth/common-account.
#
# The q-sunshine TLS protocol supplies exactly one username/password pair. Its
# helper sets PAM_RHOST from the kernel-observed remote IP but rejects a second
# interactive PAM prompt (OTP/challenge/password-change); do not enable an
# interactive-MFA module here unless it has a compatible non-interactive mode.
# The q-sunshine-auth@VMID service additionally requires its own explicit
# QSUNSHINE_AUTH_ALLOWED_USERS allowlist, so PAM success alone never grants
# access to every VM.
#
# On a non-Debian layout, replace these includes with the site's equivalent
# auth/account policy. Do not add a broad bypass rule just for q-sunshine.

@include common-auth
@include common-account
